 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-* **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the size of the GPU texture cache and CPU cache [S1]. The GPU cache is a preallocated 3D texture [S1].
-* **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-* **Scaling:** The method is designed for out-of-core rendering, scaling to datasets larger than GPU memory by using the CPU cache and asynchronous loading [S1].
-* **Strengths:** Hides out-of-core data access latency; reduces GPU stalls; adapts to view-dependent visibility [S1].
-* **Limitations:** Requires asynchronous loading infrastructure; cache management complexity; dependent on the effectiveness of the emptiness check [S1].
-* **When to use:** Interactive out-of-core volume rendering where data exceeds GPU memory and latency must be hidden [S1].
-* **When not to use:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Latency Hiding:** Relies on asynchronous data access requests performed by a separate thread to hide out-of-core disk I/O latency [S1].
+*   **Cache Efficiency:** Uses LRU policy for both CPU (system memory) and GPU (texture) caches. The CPU cache acts as a level-2 cache, reducing disk accesses for recently used blocks [S1].
+*   **Update Frequency:** Due to temporal and spatial coherence, the number of updated nodes per frame is generally small, allowing efficient incremental updates via `glTexSubImage` [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance depends on the effectiveness of the sorting heuristic (screen-space size) and the accuracy of the empty-node determination. If visibility culling is not integrated, occluded zones may be unnecessarily refined [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in the paper "A single-pass GPU ray casting framework for interactive out-of-core volume rendering" by Vic et al. (2008) [S1]. It is part of a framework designed to reduce GPU stalls by exploiting feedback from the renderer to avoid refinement and data loading of occluded zones [S1]. The approach builds on earlier work in octree traversal and cache management [S1].
+This method was introduced in the context of interactive out-of-core volume rendering frameworks, specifically designed to reduce GPU stalls by exploiting renderer feedback to avoid loading occluded zones [S1]. It builds upon earlier work on empty-space skipping and summed-area tables for opacity estimation [S1].
 
